  - seconds: 1199
    speaker: Unnamed Citizen
    text: An attendee asked about the components of the district's revenue and the
      status of a potential education foundation. Dr. Prince explained that revenue
      includes state aid and other sources, but the fund balance (savings) is depleted.
      She noted the foundation is in progress but will not solve the immediate operating
      budget crisis.
  - seconds: 1367
    speaker: Unnamed Citizen
    text: A citizen inquired about current elementary class sizes and what they might
      become under reconfiguration. Dr. Prince stated current sizes range from 10
      to 24, and the model for reconfiguration would aim for 18-19 in K-2 and 22-23
      in grades 3-4, staying within board guidelines.
  - seconds: 1449
    speaker: Unnamed Citizen
    text: A question was raised about the impact of elementary reconfiguration on
      busing costs. Dr. Prince responded that while a detailed analysis hasn't been
      done, the district already runs complex busing routes and any change would require
      a holistic review of the entire transportation system.
  - seconds: 1527
    speaker: Rosemary
    text: A board member clarified that proposed class sizes would remain within board
      guidelines and asked why the health insurance cost projection was raised to
      12%. Dr. Prince explained it is a more conservative estimate based on informal
      polling of what other districts are budgeting, aiming to avoid a last-minute
      shortfall.
  - seconds: 1658
    speaker: Unnamed Citizen
    text: An attendee asked why the idea of consolidating the two middle schools was
      no longer on the list of options for FY27. Dr. Prince clarified that the timeline
      was not realistic due to external factors like permitting and traffic studies,
      but the idea is still being explored for the following year.
  - seconds: 1727
    speaker: Unnamed Citizen
    text: A citizen asked if other districts have faced similar cuts and how they
      handled them. Dr. Prince confirmed this is a common issue, with reconfiguration
      and school closures being the primary strategies used elsewhere, though South
      Portland is hesitant to close a building due to future space needs for pre-K.
